    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "therapists just say everything is a \"process\" or \"stages of grief\" but it doesn't change the fact that the person who made me *me* is dead. i look in the mirror and i don't even recognize the person looking back. it's like i'm already dead, just walking around in a body that doesn't belong to anyone anymore. how do you rebuild something when the foundation is just... gone?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the foundation is gone, you don't rebuild on it; you acknowledge the rubble and start finding a new, smaller patch of ground to stand on. This isn't about finding the old self, because that self was tethered to something that is now gone. Instead, focus on the smallest, most immediate needs—a small act of self-care, a single quiet moment—as a way to anchor yourself in the present moment. Small, deliberate acts of self-redefinition are how you begin to build a new, sustainable structure, one tiny beam at a time.",
      "source": "target",
